69cbe89fb66b015db9649674.body:
The Boston Harbor Islands National Recreation Area (BOHA), situated in an urban harbor, is considered at high risk to the impacts of climate change including sea level rise (SLR), erosion from coastal storms, biological invasion, and increased sea surface temperature (SST). This series of islands represent the only coastal drumlin archipelago in the U.S. that support important ecological, environmental, and cultural resources, which are managed by the National Park Service and partners. The intertidal zone of the inner islands of BOHA consist of a unique geological intertidal habitat known as mixed coarse substrate. Mixed coarse substrates host a broad range of coastal invertebrates and algae species that serve as food and shelter for other marine and coastal organisms such as sea and shorebirds, fishes, and marine mammals. However, a reliable and repeatable biodiversity monitoring framework to assess and track changes in biodiversity and baseline conditions in mixed coarse substrate habitat did not exist previous to this study. Considering the irreplaceable value of this region and the growing risks posed by climate change, such methodology is crucial to support coastal adaptation actions and decision making in the future.<br>
<br>
To address this need, this project aimed to develop and deploy a new biodiversity monitoring protocol through the following objectives: 1) test and calibrate existing protocols to quantify and track the unique biotic assemblages associated with mixed coarse substrate habitats, 2) collect current baseline data of environmental characteristics and biodiversity at key sites, and 3) develop a novel monitoring framework that can be implemented by the NPS and its partners moving forward. Overall, a combination of visual, molecular, and trap-based methods were field-tested across three sites on a single island (Georges Island) during Year-1 of the project (2022), and a final calibrated framework was used to collect a baseline biodiversity dataset across five islands and ten sites during Year-2 (2023). Corresponding environmental data was collected across target islands, recording finescale intertidal water temperatures and salinity patterns. Results provide a suite of methods and current information to support the NPS and BOHA partners in site selection and environmental assessments as adaptation projects are pursued in response to ongoing climate change impacts. This project builds upon a previous study funded by the National Resource Preservation Program (NRPP: FY20 project, A pilot biodiversity inventory and monitoring protocol in support of coastal adaptation projects in tidal and nearshore subtidal habitats of Boston Harbor Islands), which synthesized historical information as the foundation for scope of work described here.&nbsp;

