Abstract (from <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/nclimate3375">Nature Climate Change</a>):&nbsp;In coming decades, warmer winters are likely to ease range constraints on many cold-limited forest insects<a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/nclimate3375#ref-CR1" style="vertical-align: baseline; background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 102, 153); overflow-wrap: break-word; word-break: break-word;" title="Paradis, A., Elkinton, J., Hayhoe, K. &amp; Buonaccorsi, J. Role of winter temperature and climate change on the survival and future range expansion of the hemlock woolly adelgid (Adelges tsugae) in eastern North America. Mitig. Adapt. Strateg. Glob. Change 13, 541–554 (2008).">1</a>,<a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/nclimate3375#ref-CR2" style="vertical-align: baseline; background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 102, 153); overflow-wrap: break-word; word-break: break-word;" title="Ogden, N. H. et al. Climate change and the potential for range expansion of the Lyme disease vector Ixodes scapularis in Canada. Int. J. Parasitol. 36, 63–70 (2006).">2</a>,<a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/nclimate3375#ref-CR3" style="vertical-align: baseline; background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 102, 153); overflow-wrap: break-word; word-break: break-word;" title="Rochlin, I., Ninivaggi, D. V., Hutchinson, M. L. &amp; Farajollahi, A. Climate change and range expansion of the Asian tiger mosquito (Aedes albopictus) in Northeastern USA: implications for public health practitioners. PLoS ONE 8, 1–9 (2013).">3</a>,<a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/nclimate3375#ref-CR4" style="vertical-align: baseline; background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 102, 153); overflow-wrap: break-word; word-break: break-word;" title="Ungerer, M. J., Ayres, M. P. &amp; Lombardero, M. J. Climate and the northern distribution limits of Dendroctonus frontalis Zimmermann (Coleoptera: Scolytidae). J. Biogeogr. 26, 1133–1145 (1999).">4</a>,<a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/nclimate3375#ref-CR5" style="vertical-align: baseline; background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 102, 153); overflow-wrap: break-word; word-break: break-word;" title="Williams, D. W. &amp; Liebhold, A. M. Climate change and the outbreak ranges of two North American bark beetles. Agric. For. Entomol. 4, 87–99 (2002).">5</a>. Recent unprecedented expansion of the southern pine beetle (SPB,&nbsp;<em>Dendroctonus frontalis</em>) into New Jersey, New York and Connecticut in concert with warming annual temperature minima highlights the risk that this insect pest poses to the pine forests of the northern United States and Canada under continued climate change<a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/nclimate3375#ref-CR6" style="vertical-align: baseline; background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 102, 153); overflow-wrap: break-word; word-break: break-word;" title="Dodds, K. J. &amp; Krist, F. Destructive Southern Pine Beetle Begins Invading Northeastern Forests – GIS Story Map (2015); 
                    http://usfs.maps.arcgis.com/apps/MapTour/index.html?appid=3dfab0794d5e4f0f886590da8765f4f5

                  ">6</a>. Here we present projections of northward expansion in SPB-suitable climates using a statistical bioclimatic range modelling approach and current-generation general circulation model output under Representative Concentration Pathways 4.5 and 8.5. Results show that by the middle of the twenty-first century, the climate is likely to be suitable for SPB expansion into vast areas of previously unaffected forests throughout the northeastern United States and into southeastern Canada. This scenario would pose a significant economic and ecological risk to the affected regions, including disruption of local ecosystem services<a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/nclimate3375#ref-CR7" style="vertical-align: baseline; background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 102, 153); overflow-wrap: break-word; word-break: break-word;" title="Mikkelson, K. M. et al. Bark beetle infestation impacts on nutrient cycling, water quality and interdependent hydrological effects. Biogeochemistry 115, 1–21 (2013).">7</a>, shifts in forest structure<a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/nclimate3375#ref-CR8" style="vertical-align: baseline; background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 102, 153); overflow-wrap: break-word; word-break: break-word;" title="Harrington, T. B., Xu, M. G. &amp; Edwards, M. B. Structural characteristics of late-successional pine-hardwood forest following recent infestation by southern pine beetle in the Georgia Piedmont, USA. Nat. Areas J. 20, 360–365 (2000).">8</a>, and threats to native biodiversity<a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/nclimate3375#ref-CR9" style="vertical-align: baseline; background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 102, 153); overflow-wrap: break-word; word-break: break-word;" title="Wagner, D., Nelson, M. W. &amp; Schweitzer, D. F. Shrubland Lepidoptera of southern New England and southeastern New York: ecology, conservation, and management. For. Ecol. Manag. 185, 95–112 (2003).">9</a>.

